Terminate g_app_config with NULL, not the string "0".

sconfig_load() and its siblings walk g_app_config until a NULL entry,
but the array ends with the string "0", which is a valid pointer. When
no config file is present every consumer runs past the end of the array
and dereferences whatever follows it.

The shipped shared library masks this: the bytes after the array happen
to be benign. Linked statically into another binary it reads garbage and
crashes in sv_init -> sconfig_load -> sfs_open -> smem_strlen.

diff --git a/sunvox_lib/main/sunvox_lib.cpp b/sunvox_lib/main/sunvox_lib.cpp
index 40c31c4..1645ad4 100644
--- a/sunvox_lib/main/sunvox_lib.cpp
+++ b/sunvox_lib/main/sunvox_lib.cpp
@@ -30,7 +30,7 @@
     #define SUNVOX_EXPORT
 #endif
 
-const char* g_app_config[] = { "1:/sunvox_dll_config.ini", "2:/sunvox_dll_config.ini", "0" };
+const char* g_app_config[] = { "1:/sunvox_dll_config.ini", "2:/sunvox_dll_config.ini", NULL };
 const char* g_app_log = "3:/sunvox_dll_log.txt";
 const char* g_app_name = "SunVox Library";
 const char* g_app_name_short = "SunVox Library";
